---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `Fusion Lamination vs Glue — Inflatable SUP Seam Technology | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Technical comparison of fusion lamination (RF welding) vs adhesive bonding for inflatable SUP seams. Strength, durability, manufacturing speed, and quality indicators.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Fusion Lamination vs Glue — SUP Seam Technology',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">Fusion Lamination vs Glue</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Materials</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">8 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Fusion Lamination vs Glue</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      The seam bonding method is the single most important factor in inflatable SUP durability. Fusion lamination and
      adhesive bonding produce fundamentally different joint characteristics.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>Fusion Lamination (RF Welding)</h2>
      <p>
        Radio-frequency (RF) welding uses electromagnetic energy at 27.12 MHz to excite PVC molecules at the seam
        interface. The molecules vibrate and generate heat, melting the PVC layers together. When the RF field is
        removed, the material cools and solidifies into a single homogeneous joint. The bond strength equals or exceeds
        the parent material — the seam is stronger than the surrounding PVC.
      </p>
      <h3>Process Parameters</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Frequency: 27.12 MHz (ISM band)</li><li>Temperature at weld interface: 160-200°C (320-392°F)</li><li>
          Weld time: 3-8 seconds per seam segment
        </li><li>Pressure: 40-80 PSI at the electrode</li><li>Cooling time: 2-4 seconds under maintained pressure</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Advantages</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Molecular bond — no adhesive layer to fail</li><li>Consistent, repeatable weld quality</li><li>
          No curing time — immediate full strength
        </li><li>No VOCs or solvent emissions</li><li>Higher temperature resistance than adhesive bonds</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Adhesive Bonding</h2>
      <p>
        Adhesive bonding uses two-component polyurethane or contact cement to join PVC layers. The adhesive is applied
        to both surfaces, allowed to dry to tack, then pressed together under rollers or in a laminating press. Full
        cure takes 24-72 hours depending on temperature and humidity. Adhesive bonds rely on mechanical interlocking and
        surface chemistry rather than molecular fusion.
      </p>
      <h3>Advantages</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Lower equipment investment</li><li>Can bond dissimilar materials (PVC to fabric, etc.)</li><li>
          More forgiving of surface contamination
        </li><li>Lower energy consumption per board</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Limitations</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Adhesive layer is the weakest point — peel failure mode</li><li>
          Vulnerable to heat — adhesives soften at 60-80°C
        </li><li>Curing time slows production</li><li>Quality depends on surface preparation consistency</li><li>
          Solvent-based adhesives emit VOCs
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Quality Indicators</h2>
      <p>
        To identify fusion-laminated vs glue-bonded boards: fusion seams show a smooth, uniform bead line at the rail
        edge with no visible adhesive squeeze-out. Glue-bonded seams may show dried adhesive residue. On fusion seams,
        the rail and deck PVC blend together seamlessly. On glue-bonded boards, you can sometimes separate the layers at
        the seam edge with fingernail pressure — a sign of a weak bond.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
